                <pb facs="0493" n="235" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg235"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1571</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-14T18:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 14, 1915, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received September 15, 8 a.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2800. Your 2103, September 11, 6 p. m., taken up informally with the Foreign
                    Office which advises me that reference in Foreign Office note was not meant to
                    mean “Holland alone” but that action will apply to all neutral countries and
                    ports concerning goods of German, Austrian, and Turkish origin. I urged upon the
                    Foreign Office importance of receipt of the information referred to and was
                    informed that matter was still under consideration by the French Government but
                    that the Foreign Office was pressing them for an answer and promised to let the
                    Department know as soon as they possibly could.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
